diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep of the dc_clos_bgp fabric found no fault, past or present, consistent with the reported ticket.

**Affected component:** None identified. The full control plane (FRR watchfrr/zebra/bgpd) and data plane are healthy on all five routers.

**Mechanism:** The user reported that pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) could not reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) and attributed the cause to "a spine box dying." Live end-to-end reachability (Phase 1) shows 0% packet loss in both directions right now. Phase A confirmed that every interface across all seven devices is UP with no duplicate MACs, no ACL drop rules, and no addressing anomalies. Phase B confirmed that both spine routers (spine_router_0_0 ASN 65100 and spine_router_0_1 ASN 65101) have all three BGP sessions Established — to the super-spine and to both leaves — and the super-spine has both spine sessions Established. Every prefix is correctly advertised. Phase B tc_snapshot shows no bandwidth shaping or corruption on any interface. Phase D pressure_sweep found no stress tools or resource pressure on any device.

**Localization:** No device is implicated.

**Root cause:** There is no active or traceable fault in the fabric; all evidence is consistent with a past transient (e.g., a brief BGP convergence event after a container restart) that has since self-resolved, leaving no current mechanism, flap process, or historical counter anomaly.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. If the user experiences further disruption, capture packet-loss evidence and syslog/BGP event logs at the time of the incident for root-cause analysis.
=== END ===